{
  // ⚠ NAMED `tsconfig.app.json`, NOT `tsconfig.json`, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
  //
  // This repo is on **TypeScript 7.0.2**, which is the Go port. It removed the classic
  // compiler API from the JS package: `ts.sys`, `ts.readConfigFile` and
  // `ts.parseJsonConfigFileContent` are all `undefined` (verified 2026-08-11).
  // `@expo/cli` reads the project's tsconfig through exactly those three, in
  // `utils/tsconfig/evaluateTsConfig.ts`, on the Metro-config path that BOTH `expo start` and
  // `expo export` go through. With a `tsconfig.json` present, every Expo command dies with:
  //     TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'getCurrentDirectory')
  // With no `tsconfig.json`, the CLI skips that step and Metro runs.
  //
  // So the app is typechecked against THIS file (`pnpm -C apps/manager typecheck` passes `-p`)
  // and Expo never sees a tsconfig it cannot parse. Nothing is lost: that code path exists to
  // read `compilerOptions.paths`, and this app declares none.
  //
  // **This is a workaround, not a fix, and the real decision is not a session's to make** —
  // either the repo carries a second, 5.x TypeScript for `apps/manager` (a `18 §15` lockfile
  // question: `typescript` is allowlisted, two versions of it is a governance call), or Expo
  // gains TS7 support upstream. Whoever revisits it: renaming this file back is the test.
  //
  // Its own program, on purpose — see the `exclude` note in the root tsconfig. React Native's
  // ambient globals (notably `setTimeout` returning `number`) are incompatible with
  // `@types/node`, so this app cannot be typechecked in the same pass as the services.
  //
  // `types: []` is the other half of that isolation: it stops `@types/node` being pulled in
  // implicitly from the workspace root, which would recreate the exact clash in reverse.
  "extends": "../../packages/config/tsconfig.base.json",
  "include": ["src"],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": [],
    // "DOM" is what Expo's own `tsconfig.base` uses, and it is NOT a claim that a DOM exists.
    // It is how the web-standard globals Hermes implements get types at all — `TextEncoder`
    // (which `packages/domain`'s audit hash and payload hash both construct) and
    // `crypto.getRandomValues` (which `@noble/hashes` needs for the PIN salt) live there.
    // ⚠ It also types `document` and `window`, which do NOT exist on this platform. TypeScript
    // will not stop you reaching for them here; Hermes will, at runtime.
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM"],
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true
  }
}
